  "body": {
    "id": "lem:spectral-bound-moments",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "The universal constant \\(C_0<\\infty\\) may be chosen so that every \\((G_n,Y_n)\\in\\mathcal M_n(d;B,c)\\) satisfies \\[L_n\\le C_0\\lambda_n,\\qquad n^2\\sigma_n^2\\le L_nH_{2,n},\\qquad \\mathbb E_U\\widehat H_{2,n}=H_{2,n},\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}_U(\\widehat H_{2,n})\\le2C_0Bn\\lambda_n.\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "C_0",
      "G_n",
      "Y_n",
      "\\mathcal M_n(d;B,c)",
      "n",
      "d",
      "B",
      "c",
      "L_n",
      "\\lambda_n",
      "\\sigma_n^2",
      "H_{2,n}",
      "\\mathbb E_U",
      "\\widehat H_{2,n}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:treated-fourth-moment",
      "ass:control-fourth-moment",
      "def:spectral-bound",
      "lem:perron-prefix-operator",
      "lem:exact-forward-doob",
      "lem:prefix-flip-moments"
    ],
    "justification": "This proposed squared-endpoint reduction would turn the published CGD operator inequality into uniform moment control for the observable scale.",
    "gap": "Propositions 5.3--5.4 of \\cite{KandirosPipisDaskalakisHarshaw2024CGD} establish additive and ratio consistency for the spectral variance-bound estimator, respectively, but not this fixed-class quantitative package.",
    "consumer": "The inequalities supply the denominator and numerator controls for uniform ratio consistency.",
    "proof_tex": "For arbitrary deterministic real arrays \\(x=(x_i)\\) and \\(y=(y_i)\\), define\n\\[\n F_n(x,y)=\\sum_i\\left\\{x_i\\frac{\\mathbf1(E_{n,i,1})}{q_{n,i}}\n -y_i\\frac{\\mathbf1(E_{n,i,0})}{q_{n,i}}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThe exact decomposition proved in \\(\\mathrm{lem:exact\\text{-}forward\\text{-}doob}\\) applies algebraically with endpoints \\(x_i,y_i\\).  Put \\(w_i=|x_i|+|y_i|\\) and \\(v=\\lambda_nw+\\mathsf P_nw\\).  The pointwise coefficient bounds in the proof of \\(\\mathrm{lem:prefix\\text{-}flip\\text{-}moments}\\) give\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Var}_U\\{F_n(x,y)\\}=\\mathbb E_UQ_n(x,y)\n \\le32p_n\\|v\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nBy \\(\\mathrm{lem:perron\\text{-}prefix\\text{-}operator}\\),\n\\[\n \\|v\\|_2\\le2\\lambda_n\\|w\\|_2,\\qquad\n \\|w\\|_2^2\\le2\\sum_i(x_i^2+y_i^2).\n\\]\nSince \\(p_n=(2\\lambda_n)^{-1}\\), enlargement of the universal constant gives\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Var}_U\\{F_n(x,y)\\}\n \\le C_0\\lambda_n\\sum_i(x_i^2+y_i^2).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\theta=(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n\\), then the sign convention \\(s_1=1,s_0=-1\\) gives \\(F_n(x,y)=\\theta^{\\mathsf T}W_n\\), and hence\n\\[\n \\theta^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf V_n\\theta\\le C_0\\lambda_n\\|\\theta\\|_2^2\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^{2n}\\).  The Rayleigh-quotient characterization of the largest eigenvalue of the symmetric positive semidefinite matrix \\(\\mathbf V_n\\) yields \\(L_n\\le C_0\\lambda_n\\).\n\nFor the actual endpoints, \\(F_n=nT_n\\) and its coefficient vector has squared norm \\(H_{2,n}\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n n^2\\sigma_n^2=\\operatorname{Var}_U(F_n)\n \\le L_nH_{2,n}.\n\\]\nEndpoint realization on \\(E_{n,i,k}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\widehat H_{2,n}=F_n((a_{n,i}^2)_i,(-b_{n,i}^2)_i).\n\\]\nThe expectation identity from the same Horvitz--Thompson algebra is thus\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_U\\widehat H_{2,n}=\\sum_i\\{a_{n,i}^2-(-b_{n,i}^2)\\}=H_{2,n}.\n\\]\nApplying the just-proved variance inequality to these transformed endpoints and then using the two fourth-moment assumptions gives\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Var}_U(\\widehat H_{2,n})\n \\le C_0\\lambda_n\\sum_i(a_{n,i}^4+b_{n,i}^4)\n \\le2C_0Bn\\lambda_n.\n\\]\nOne common enlargement of \\(C_0\\) proves the lemma.",
